-- Extensions the schema relies on.
--
-- IDEMPOTENT AND RE-RUNNABLE, like every file in this directory: the runner
-- keeps no applied-migrations table and replays every `*.up.sql` in filename
-- order, so each statement must be a no-op the second time round.
--
-- Conventions used by every table below (the reasoning is written once here):
--   * `id UUID PRIMARY KEY`, generated by the application as UUIDv7. v7 is
--     time-ordered, so new rows append to the right edge of the PK index
--     (no random page splits, far less WAL and bloat than v4) and the id
--     doubles as the keyset-pagination cursor: `WHERE id > $token ORDER BY id`.
--   * `state TEXT` ('ACTIVE' | 'DELETED') is the AEP-135 soft-delete marker,
--     only on resources that can be undeleted. Uniqueness among live rows is
--     a PARTIAL unique index (`WHERE state = 'ACTIVE'`), so a deleted row
--     never blocks reuse of its key and the index stays small.
--   * Enumerations are plain TEXT validated in the application, same as the
--     approved global-settings schema.
--   * No trigger sets `update_time`: the application does, in the same UPDATE
--     statement, so writes pay for no extra trigger call.
--   * Every list endpoint is `WHERE <parent> = $1 AND id > $2 ORDER BY id`,
--     so child tables carry `(parent_id, id)` — one index, no sort step.
--   * Index only what a query needs. Each extra index is paid for on every
--     write; tables that take bulk writes (import rows, SKU prices) carry the
--     minimum.

CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS ltree;
